From: Deepak Kumar Singh <quic_deesin@quicinc.com>
Subject: [PATCH] rpmsg: glink: Make glink smem interrupt wakeup capable

There are certain usecases which require glink interrupt to be wakeup
capable. For example if handset is in sleep state and usb charger is
plugged in, dsp wakes up and sends glink interrupt to host for glink
pmic channel communication. Glink is suppose to wakeup host processor
completely for further glink data handling. IRQF_NO_SUSPEND does not
gurantee complete wakeup, system may again enter sleep after interrupt
handling and glink data may not be handled by pmic client driver.

To ensure data handling by client configure glink smem device as wakeup
source and attach glink interrupt as wakeup irq. Remove IRQF_NO_SUSPEND
flag as it is no longer required.

Signed-off-by: Deepak Kumar Singh <quic_deesin@quicinc.com>

[armada] Imported to replace 0504-mailbox-qcom-ipcc-mask-summary-irq-
for-suspend-to-ram.patch. That patch let SM8550/kalama enter deep
suspend-to-RAM but masked the IPCC summary IRQ, which also killed the
charger doorbell while asleep (deep-suspend XOR charging). Making the
glink-smem IRQ wakeup-capable is the upstream / QTI-downstream approach
used by kalama OEM kernels: a real charger-plug doorbell wakes the AP,
battmgr processes it, and the system re-suspends -- deep suspend AND
charging, no trade-off.

[armada] Implicit dependency (FMEA MEDIUM): enable_irq_wake(smem->irq)
does not arm any HW wake bit by itself -- the IPCC irqchip carries
IRQCHIP_SKIP_SET_WAKE and implements no .irq_set_wake, so this call is a
no-op at the irqchip level. The actual wake capability comes ENTIRELY
from two conditions outside this patch: (1) the IPCC summary irq is
still requested IRQF_NO_SUSPEND, and (2) SM8550 never power-collapses
the GIC in S2RAM. If either changes later -- summary irq made
suspend-capable, or GIC power-collapse enabled for S2RAM -- this wakeup
breaks SILENTLY (system goes back to 0W deep suspend with no error).
QA note: after any suspend/resume change on SM8550, confirm the wakeup
is attributed to glink-smem in /sys/kernel/debug/wakeup_sources.

[armada] Blast radius (FMEA MEDIUM): this patch is deliberately GLOBAL --
it makes the glink-smem edge wakeup-capable for every family this kernel
builds (SM8250/SM8550/SM8650/SM8750), unlike the removed 0504-mask patch
below, which only touched qcom,sm8550-ipcc/qcom,sm8750-ipcc via
of_device_is_compatible. Before a multi-device release, run a suspend
smoke test on at least one non-SM8550 device (RP5/Mini/Flip2 = SM8250,
Pocket S2/FIT = SM8650) to confirm no regression there.

Origin: https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org/msg2569356.html
--- a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_smem.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_smem.c
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
 #include <linux/list.h>
+#include <linux/pm_wakeirq.h>
 
 #include <linux/rpmsg/qcom_glink.h>
 
@@ -306,8 +307,7 @@
 
 	smem->irq = of_irq_get(smem->dev.of_node, 0);
 	ret = devm_request_irq(&smem->dev, smem->irq, qcom_glink_smem_intr,
-			       IRQF_NO_SUSPEND | IRQF_NO_AUTOEN,
-			       "glink-smem", smem);
+			       IRQF_NO_AUTOEN, "glink-smem", smem);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(&smem->dev, "failed to request IRQ\n");
 		goto err_put_dev;
@@ -346,6 +346,8 @@
 
 	smem->glink = glink;
 
+	device_init_wakeup(dev, true);
+	dev_pm_set_wake_irq(dev, smem->irq);
 	enable_irq(smem->irq);
 
 	return smem;
@@ -365,6 +367,8 @@
 	struct qcom_glink *glink = smem->glink;
 
 	disable_irq(smem->irq);
+	dev_pm_clear_wake_irq(&smem->dev);
+	device_init_wakeup(&smem->dev, false);
 
 	qcom_glink_native_remove(glink);
 
